      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"contradictions\": [\n    \"Humour rule vs beat 5. Manifest v2: the humour “does not work on anyone who has been in their kitchen.” In beat 5 Sarah is standing in the kitchen and laughs. In beat 14 she does not. The chapter's turn depends on a threshold the Manifest never defines: first visit, second visit, or something else. Read literally, beat 5 breaks the rule.\",\n    \"Precision vs choice of hiding place. The protagonist prices other people's catastrophes for a living. They move the letter from under a fruit bowl that Sarah might glance at into the one container in the house she reaches into every morning. Then they lock it with her property inside. Beat 12 is the obvious outcome and needs one step of foresight. Either the protagonist wants it found, or their precision fails at exactly the point the plot needs it to. If they want it found, that motive is not on the page and belongs to the arc after Ch 7.\",\n    \"Conservation of Stillness vs the chapter's engine. The theory says: keep perfectly still and events happen to someone else. Beats 3 and 7–10 are a chain of actions: bowl moved, envelope moved, tea lifted and replaced, drawer locked, drawer tested twice, key pocketed, bowl restored. Stillness is kept up by motion. The outcome does match the theory, because the event, the stuck drawer, lands on Sarah. But the Manifest arc runs “from maintained stillness to forced movement”, and here the movement is unforced, three chapters before the old self dies. The beats do not show which reading is intended.\",\n    \"“She does not know the drawer locks” vs beats 13–14. The Manifest states this as a standing fact. By beat 14 Sarah has watched a hand go to a pocket at the word “stuck” and has looked at that hand. If she infers a key, the Manifest line stops being true on Wednesday of Ch 4 and nothing records that. If she does not, beat 14 is a look with no content.\",\n    \"Envelope vs letter. The SSOT locks “the letter”. Beats 7–8 handle “the envelope”, which “stays folded along its creases.” An envelope folded along creases has been folded. Otherwise the object is a letter outside its envelope. Whether it has been opened or read is undefined, and the beats use both meanings.\",\n    \"Point of view at beat 19. Both characters have left. The inventory of cold kettle, dry mug and locked drawer happens with no one in the room. The Manifest frames perception through the protagonist (“They notice this about themselves”). If the narration is bound to the protagonist, beat 19 has no observer. It is the author's camera.\"\n  ],\n  \"hidden_assumptions\": [\n    \"The reader already knows something is under the fruit bowl. Beat 3 (“another inch”) and beat 10 (“where it stood before Monday”) depend on Ch 3 having shown the bowl being moved and why. If Ch 3 did not, beat 3 hides the letter so that beat 7 can reveal it. That is a surprise mechanism, which the Out of Scope line rules out in spirit.\",\n    \"The letter and Sarah arrive on the same day. The MOT fails in Ch 3, and the bowl was displaced on Monday. These are treated as either cause and effect or coincidence, and neither is examined.\",\n    \"Back-door access. In beat 6 the handle is checked twice, so the door is locked. In beat 11 Sarah lets herself in. Either the protagonist unlocks the door before 7:40 every day, a ritual that is not on the page, or Sarah holds a key. A key is a step of intimacy that neither the Manifest nor the SSOT records. “Lets herself in” quietly assumes one of these.\",\n    \"The lock was invisible on Tuesday morning. Sarah opened the drawer and registered no key in it and no keyhole. Dresser locks are usually visible. This is assumed, not established.\",\n    \"Key continuity overnight. Beat 13 needs the protagonist to be wearing the same extra layer on Wednesday morning, or to have moved the key into whatever they are wearing. Neither is shown.\",\n    \"The coat. In beat 1 it hangs over the chair. Wednesday never mentions it, and the beat 19 inventory leaves it out. A protagonist who is always cold leaves the house, and the beats do not account for the coat.\",\n    \"Ch 4 can show Sarah seeing clearly without using up her Ch 5 key line. Beat 14 assumes the look and the line will be read as two separate rungs, not one event split across two chapters.\",\n    \"The revision carries “the cost” thread. The spine run predicted Ch 4 for that thread and observed 0 of 4. There has been no run against the revised beats. The repair is assumed. The evidence does not decide this, but no evidence exists either way.\",\n    \"Handling the envelope by its edges is meant to read as avoidance with no access to the protagonist's thoughts. The gesture alone is expected to carry fifteen years of silence.\"\n  ],\n  \"edge_case_failures\": [\n    \"Early resolution of the seeing. The reader puts together beat 13 (hand to pocket) and beat 14 (Sarah looks at the hand) and concludes that Sarah knows something is being hidden and roughly where. The Ch 5 key line then lands as confirmation, not escalation. Connective tissue has answered “does Sarah see?” one chapter early. Ch 5, which is itself connective and sits before pillar 7, now has to top a confrontation that has already half happened.\",\n    \"Sarah's tea is held hostage. Her tea is locked in with the letter, and under the Frozen Constant the letter stays locked until Ch 13. Each branch spends tension or bends a constant. (a) Ch 5's kitchen mornings address the drawer: every mention escalates, and the confrontation accelerates before Ch 7. (b) They ignore it: the reader notices, and tension leaks out as implausibility. (c) The car is repaired at the end of Ch 5 and the tea stays: Sarah's object lives with the letter for eight more chapters, which the SSOT does not record, and it will be in the drawer at pillar Ch 13. (d) The drawer is opened to return the tea: this tests whether “locked from Ch 4 to Ch 13” survives a brief unlock.\",\n    \"Ch 4 is a complete arc. It contains a claim (beat 2), a threat (beat 3), an act (beats 7–10), a consequence (beat 12), a witness (beat 14), a withdrawal (beat 17) and a closing still life (beat 19), all in two days. The closing tableau resolves into a symbol instead of leaving the question open. The lock is also the most active concealment in Act 1. Together these make the drawer compete with Ch 7 as the moment the Lie peaks, so the reader feels a pillar in a chapter the SSOT says is not one.\"\n  ],\n  \"structural_debt\": \"The middle drawer is now load-bearing for ten chapters and holds two objects. The SSOT records only one. Sarah's tea makes her a co-tenant of the secret without her knowledge, and from Ch 6 she is absent. The beats do not say whether the tea is how her absence is felt, or simply an untracked prop that will sit in the drawer at Ch 13. Ch 5 inherits four obligations: the daily stuck drawer, the key line partly spent by beat 14, the tea that cannot be returned, and the back-door key question. What is withheld, and whether it earns its place: (1) The letter's contents and the fifteen-year decision. This earns its place only if Ch 13 pays it off, and these beats commit Ch 13 to opening the drawer in front of Sarah's tea. (2) The protagonist's inner life, withheld completely because every beat is exterior. That withholding is not earned. It moves the chapter's meaning onto objects, and then beat 19 lists those objects for the reader in a scene nobody is present to see. The pacing serves the reader through beats 11–17, where silence carries the pressure. It serves the writer at beats 3 and 19, which are a staged concealment and a curated closing image. The cost thread was evidenced at zero before the revision, and no evidence has been gathered since.\",\n  \"voice_violation\": \"Manifest: “Notices details others miss but refuses to act on them.” Ch 4 reverses both halves. The protagonist acts: bowl, envelope, tea, key, drawer test, handle check. And they notice nothing on the page. The noticing has been handed to Sarah: the hand in beat 14, the mug in beat 17. The Manifest says they notice their own body (dry hands), yet the hand going to the pocket in beat 13 goes unregistered. Either they miss their own tell, which contradicts their self-observation, or they catch it and the beats hide it. The humour is on spec. “Raise your premium”, “Act of God” and “Only on Wednesdays” use the insurance register and deflect, and the second joke after a failed first one matches “slightly too quick.” Precision appears only as compulsion (two double-checks), which fits Ch 2's armour rule. So precision is present as behaviour but absent as voice. Secondary point: Sarah's opening line, “Squatter's rights”, borrows the protagonist's legal-and-property joke register. That blurs the two voices on the first morning they share the kitchen, when her spec is direct questions that sound casual.\"\n}\n```"
